  "conceptLabel": "Factoring & the Zero-Product Property — Honors Algebra II",
  "flagTitle": "Caution: three moves that are NOT legal here",
  "flagIntro": "The Zero-Product Property only applies to a product equal to zero. Before you split an equation into two factors, check that the structure is genuinely a product — and that you are not 'canceling' a shared term that was never a factor.",
  "cautions": [
    {
      "kind": "illegal_transfer",
      "title": "Do NOT cancel a term out of a SUM",
      "detail": "Cancellation is legal only for common FACTORS multiplying the whole expression. A shared term inside a sum or difference is not a factor, so dividing it out changes the solution set. This is the single most common lost-solution error on the unit test.",
      "wrongExample": "x^2 - 5x = 3x  →  x^2 = 8  (divide both sides by x)",
      "rightExample": "x^2 - 5x - 3x = 0 → x(x - 8) = 0 → x = 0 or x = 8",
      "exampleNote": "Dividing by x silently discards the root x = 0. Move every term to one side, factor, THEN apply the Zero-Product Property.",
      "ruleRef": "Zero-Product Property"
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_example",
      "title": "x^2 + 4 is NOT a difference of squares",
      "detail": "A sum of squares does not factor over the integers. Students reflexively write x^2 + 4 = (x + 2)(x - 2), which is false by FOIL and would introduce imaginary roots into a real-valued modeling problem.",
      "wrongExample": "x^2 + 4 = (x + 2)(x - 2)",
      "rightExample": "x^2 + 4 is prime over ℤ[x]",
      "exampleNote": "Only a^2 - b^2 factors; the sign must be a MINUS between the two squares.",
      "ruleRef": "Difference of Squares"
    },
    {
      "kind": "exception",
      "title": "GCF factoring DOES survive the zero set — and it may ADD a root",
      "detail": "Factoring out a greatest common factor is a legal transformation of a polynomial's zeros, but the factored form can reveal a root (the zero from the GCF itself) that the unfactored form hides. Always extract the GCF first.",
      "wrongExample": "3x^2 - 12x = 0 → 3(x^2 - 4x) → read only the quadratic part",
      "rightExample": "3x(x - 4) = 0 → x = 0 or x = 4",
      "exampleNote": "The factor x contributed by the GCF carries the root x = 0.",
      "ruleRef": "GCF Extraction"
    },
    {
      "kind": "pitfall",
      "title": "Domain restrictions are NOT optional in rational equations",
      "detail": "When you clear denominators by multiplying both sides, you are implicitly assuming those denominators are nonzero. A solution that makes any original denominator zero is extraneous and must be rejected, even though it satisfies the cleared equation.",
      "wrongExample": "(x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) = 0 → x = 2 and x = -2 both accepted",
      "rightExample": "x + 2 = 0 with x ≠ 2 → x = -2 only",
      "exampleNote": "x = 2 is a hole in the domain, not a solution. State the restriction before you solve.",
      "ruleRef": "Clearing Denominators"
    }
  ],
  "footerNote": "Exam reminder: on the non-calculator section you must show the factored form AND the restricted domain line before writing the solution set. A correct numeric answer with an illegal algebraic step earns no credit."
